    <dcterms:title>Vikram and the Vampire: Classic Hindu Tales of Adventure, Magic, and Romance</dcterms:title>
    <dcterms:description>Wikipedia page about this book: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vetala_Panchavimshati</dcterms:description>
    <dcterms:description>Uniform Title: Vetalapañcavimsati. Hindi version (Baitala-pacisi). English</dcterms:description>
    <pgterms:marc508>Produced by Sara Vazirian and David Widger</pgterms:marc508>
    <pgterms:marc520>"Vikram and the Vampire: Classic Hindu Tales of Adventure, Magic, and Romance" is a collection of tales originally written in Sanskrit, with recensions dating to the 11th century. The work follows legendary King Vikramāditya as he attempts to capture a vetala—a vampire spirit inhabiting dead bodies and hanging from a tree. Each time the king seizes the creature, it tells a story ending with a riddle. If Vikrama answers correctly, the vampire escapes back to its tree, forcing him to start over. Through twenty-five attempts, this cycle reveals both entertainment and danger, as a dark plot gradually unfolds. (This is an automatically generated summary.)</pgterms:marc520>
    <pgterms:marc908>Reading ease score: 65.2 (8th &amp; 9th grade). Neither easy nor difficult to read.</pgterms:marc908>
